+ ### Code which was not written this way
+
+ Every handler here deals with an `Exception`, which is right for code that raises its errors.
+ A tool which drives *foreign* code, such as a compiler or a classloader, must also survive what
+ that code throws, and much of it is not an `Exception` at all: a failed assertion, an exhausted
+ stack, or a class which could not be linked. None of those ends the program, but none reaches
+ `safely` or `recover` either.
+
+ `pacify` runs a block of such code, and raises whatever it throws as a `Disturbance`, an
+ ordinary error which keeps the `throwable` it stands for:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ def depth(count: Int): Int = 1 + depth(count + 1)
+
+ val outcome = safely(pacify(depth(0)))
+ Out.println(outcome.inspect)
+
+ val message = recover:
+   case Disturbance(throwable) => throwable.getClass.getName.nn.tt
+ . protect:
+     pacify[Text](throw AssertionError("not so"))
+
+ Out.println(message)
+ ```
+
+ `pacify` takes every throwable which is not fatal to the program, and the three kinds of
+ `java.lang.Error` named above. An exhausted heap, or an interrupt, still propagates, since no
+ handler could do anything useful with it. An error raised properly inside the block is not
+ foreign: it passes through `pacify` untouched, to the handler it was raised for.
+

